WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.710
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.330
Today I would like to
talk about the plateau.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.820
Now, the plateau is a concept that
comes up often in language learning.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.430
It's something I think
we're all familiar with.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:19.410
Uh, you know, we had some initial
success in acquiring new words and

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.750
being able to understand some things and
gaining a sense of this new language.

00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:27.090
uh, I've often called it
the sort of steep, you know,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.650
blade of the hockey stick.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.390
And then we come to that period where
there's so many sort of low frequency,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.640
uh, words that we need to learn.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.750
We need to learn them because even
though they're low frequency, they come

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:45.180
up quite often and they prevent us from
being able to understand conversations,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.620
movies, books, and so forth.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.960
And it just seems like
this goes on forever.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.599
So we're in the plateau.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.040
How do we deal with the plateau?

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.340
Well, it's easy to say,
you know, just keep going.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.700
And I did do a video about Atomic
Habits where I stressed the fact that

00:01:02.705 --> 00:01:06.720
rather than worrying too much about,
you know, reaching the final goal,

00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:11.110
which might be fluency, uh, you're
better off to develop good habits.

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:15.765
Good habits that you, you know,
regularly, daily, listen, read,

00:01:15.765 --> 00:01:19.875
speak, all these things that are
gonna gradually improve your language.

00:01:20.265 --> 00:01:25.515
Another habit to try to develop is
that sense that whatever I'm doing,

00:01:25.845 --> 00:01:30.705
even if I don't think I'm progressing,
actually it is helping me improve.

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:33.824
The brain is being
exposed to the language.

00:01:34.125 --> 00:01:37.320
The brain is becoming familiar
with the language, however

00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:39.465
slowly, I am acquiring more words.

00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:44.205
So I think the image of the plateau
should be, you know, scrapped.

00:01:44.535 --> 00:01:46.815
Because a plateau suggests
that we're not going anywhere.

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:47.805
We're just flat.

00:01:48.045 --> 00:01:48.375
Okay.

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:53.715
I like the image of treading water because
treading water means you're standing,

00:01:53.715 --> 00:01:57.225
you're sort of in the water, you're moving
your feet, you're moving your hands.

00:01:57.465 --> 00:01:58.935
You're basically staying still.

00:01:58.935 --> 00:02:02.625
You're not swimming, you're not
progressing, but you're in the water.

00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:07.725
And so when we're treading water
in language learning, we are

00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:09.525
still immersed in the language.

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:11.835
We are still using our muscles.

00:02:12.705 --> 00:02:16.095
We may even be in a lake or in
the ocean, and we can look around

00:02:16.095 --> 00:02:17.595
and we can enjoy the scenery.

00:02:17.955 --> 00:02:22.935
So, and, and treading water can be,
uh, uh, unless you're, you know, you've

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:27.795
fallen off a boat and you're trying to
survive, but it, it can be, and I've,

00:02:27.795 --> 00:02:33.285
in many situations, treaded water and
enjoyed, you know, the, the coastline

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:39.405
on the Mediterranean or the coastline
on this lake or the Pacific outside,

00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:41.805
you know, west Vancouver where I live.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.175
And so it can be pleasurable.

00:02:44.655 --> 00:02:49.575
And so if we are treading water, we are
exercising our brain, our muscles, our

00:02:49.575 --> 00:02:52.275
brain, we are immersed in the language.

00:02:52.845 --> 00:02:55.185
And we need to say to ourselves,
this is where I want to be.

00:02:56.345 --> 00:02:59.175
So when I'm treading water,
I, I don't say to myself, I'd

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:00.405
rather be doing something else.

00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:06.240
How come I'm not moving forward, you
know, I can start to swim, which isn't

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:07.920
necessarily the case with languages.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.769
We can't necessarily speed up our, our
our, uh, you know, learning, uh, process.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.930
But we can enjoy where we are.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:16.740
We're immersed.

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:18.269
We're immersed in the language.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.430
We are exercising our brain.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.240
Uh, we're enjoying the scenery, we're
enjoying what we're listening to.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.130
And, and remember as again, in
Atomic Habits, you know, we can be

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:33.210
doing a lot of these little things
and incrementally one very small

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.130
step at a time, we are improving.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.190
We don't notice it until
suddenly we do notice it.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:43.110
And one thing that we do notice when we're
treading water is that we're not sinking.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.260
And obviously we wanna be treading water
somewhere where we couldn't be standing.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.490
So it has to be deep.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:54.540
It has to be deep enough so
we're actually suspended in

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.040
the water through our actions.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.680
There's something positive there
that we're doing, and so if we

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.640
can take, you know, when we feel
we're not progressing the language,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.140
we are immersed in the language.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.620
We are exercising our brains,
we are acquiring the language.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:09.840
We can enjoy it.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.240
And so I, I, I think the gist of what
I'm saying here is try to take a more

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.550
positive attitude towards that long
period where you are immersed in the

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.150
language and you're listening to things,
or you're watching things and you're not

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.770
understanding as much as you would like.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.810
And you keep forgetting words and you'd
like to be able to speak better, but

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.080
you're still immersed, you're still
in the water, you're in the lake,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.400
you're in the ocean, you are doing it.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.020
And so try to be more positive.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.260
I think a lot of people kind of feel
frustrated at their lack of progress,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.080
so I hope that's, I hope that's helpful.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:49.700
I mean, obviously you can go and look
up grammar rules when you feel like it.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:54.870
You can find other things to do and vary
your activities, but accept the fact that

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.190
when you are in that period where it seems
you're not progressing, in fact you are.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:05.310
So enjoy that sensation of treading
water and I'll leave you with a couple

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.970
of videos that I've done on this whole
issue of the plateau or that long period

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.840
where we think we're not moving forward.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.270
Thank you for listening.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:12.900
Bye for now.

